      "id": "word-un--fixed-un-tanto",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "un",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; begins un tanto, ‘somewhat’",
      "noteEn": "Inside the softening expression un tanto, un is a fixed part of the phrase rather than an article before a noun.",
      "example": { "es": "La respuesta es un tanto confusa.", "en": "The answer is somewhat confusing." },
      "tags": ["word", "fixed expression", "degree", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-tanto--fixed-un-tanto",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "tanto",
      "answerEn": "somewhat; rather (as part of un tanto)",
      "noteEn": "Tanto often concerns amount, but the fixed pair un tanto softens a description in much the same way as ‘somewhat’ or ‘rather.’",
      "example": { "es": "El final fue un tanto raro.", "en": "The ending was rather strange." },
      "tags": ["word", "fixed expression", "degree", "B1"]
    },
    { "id": "word-extraña", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "extraña", "answerEn": "strange; unusual (feminine singular)", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "adjective", "A1"] },
    { "id": "word-querida--address", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "querida", "answerEn": "darling; dear (addressing a woman)", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "term of address", "feminine"] },
    {
      "id": "word-pongámonos--lets-put-on",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "pongámonos",
      "answerEn": "let's put on; let's adopt",
      "noteEn": "Pongámonos combines the group command pongamos with nos. The final s disappears before nos, and the written accent keeps the original stress.",
      "example": { "es": "Pongámonos los abrigos.", "en": "Let's put on our coats." },
      "tags": ["word", "command", "nosotros", "attached pronoun"]
    },
    { "id": "word-mejores", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "mejores", "answerEn": "best (plural)", "noteEn": "With a possessive such as nuestras, mejores picks out the best members of a plural group. Without that kind of determiner, the same form can also mean ‘better.’", "example": { "es": "Son nuestras mejores ideas.", "en": "They are our best ideas." }, "tags": ["word", "comparison", "plural"] },
    { "id": "word-cuentas--online-accounts", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "cuentas", "answerEn": "accounts; online profiles", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "internet", "noun"] },
    { "id": "word-falsas", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "falsas", "answerEn": "fake; false (feminine plural)", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "adjective", "A1"] },
    {
      "id": "word-conectémonos--lets-connect",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "conectémonos",
      "answerEn": "let's connect; let's log on",
      "noteEn": "Conectémonos combines a group command with nos. The final s of conectemos drops before nos, and an accent is written to preserve the stress.",
      "example": { "es": "Conectémonos a la reunión.", "en": "Let's connect to the meeting." },
      "tags": ["word", "command", "internet", "attached pronoun"]
    },
    { "id": "word-foroespacio--fictional-platform", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "foroespacio", "answerEn": "ForumSpace (a fictional online forum)", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "fictional name", "internet"] },
    { "id": "word-engañemos", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "engañemos", "answerEn": "let's trick; let's deceive", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "command", "nosotros"] },
    {
      "id": "word-para--purpose-clause",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "para",
      "answerEn": "so that (before que and a desired result)",
      "noteEn": "Before que and a full event, para introduces the purpose of an action. Translate para que together as ‘so that’ or ‘in order that.’",
      "example": { "es": "Hablo despacio para que todos entiendan.", "en": "I speak slowly so that everyone understands." },
      "tags": ["word", "purpose", "connector", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-que--purpose-clause",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "que",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word; completes para que, ‘so that’",
      "noteEn": "Que completes the purpose connector para que before an event with its own person or thing carrying out the action.",
      "example": { "es": "Cierra la puerta para que no entre frío.", "en": "Close the door so that cold air does not come in." },
      "tags": ["word", "purpose", "connector", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-vea--purpose-subjunctive",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "vea",
      "answerEn": "they see; they can see (with gente as the Spanish subject)",
      "noteEn": "Vea is a special form of ver used when the seeing is a purpose or desired result rather than a stated fact. Although gente refers to people, it is grammatically singular in Spanish, so it takes the singular form vea.",
      "example": { "es": "Abro la ventana para que vea el jardín.", "en": "I open the window so that she can see the garden." },
      "tags": ["word", "purpose", "subjunctive", "A2"]
    },
    { "id": "word-imagen", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "imagen", "answerEn": "image; picture", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "noun", "A1"] },
    {
      "id": "word-ano--anus",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "ano",
      "answerEn": "anus",
      "noteEn": "Ano without an accent or ñ is an anatomical word. Do not confuse it with año, ‘year,’ which has the distinct letter ñ.",
      "example": { "es": "El médico examinó el ano.", "en": "The doctor examined the anus." },
      "tags": ["word", "anatomy", "spelling contrast"]
    },
    { "id": "word-dilatado--distended", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "dilatado", "answerEn": "dilated; distended (masculine singular)", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "adjective", "anatomy"] },
    { "id": "word-viejos", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "viejos", "answerEn": "old (masculine plural)", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "adjective", "A1"] },
    {
      "id": "word-tiempos--past-era",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "tiempos",
      "answerEn": "times; days (a past period)",
      "noteEn": "In expressions about an earlier era, tiempos means the times or days in which something used to happen, not measured timings.",
      "example": { "es": "Eran otros tiempos.", "en": "Those were different times." },
      "tags": ["word", "time", "past era"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-un-tanto",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "un tanto + descripción",
      "questionEn": "How can you soften a description to mean ‘somewhat’ or ‘rather’?",
      "answerEn": "Put un tanto before the describing word.",
      "noteEn": "Un tanto makes a description less absolute. It is often slightly more measured or formal than using un poco, but both can mean ‘somewhat.’ The description still changes normally to match what it describes.",
      "example": { "es": "La explicación es un tanto larga.", "en": "The explanation is somewhat long." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "degree", "softening", "B1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-nosotros-command-with-nos",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "orden de nosotros + nos unido al final",
      "questionEn": "How do you say ‘let's…’ with an action that also uses nos?",
      "answerEn": "Attach nos to the group-command form, remove the command's final s, and add a written accent when needed to keep the stress.",
      "noteEn": "For an action such as sentarse, start with the group command sentemos. Before attaching nos, drop the final s: sentemos + nos becomes sentémonos. The attached nos still refers to the group that includes the speaker.",
      "example": { "es": "Sentémonos aquí.", "en": "Let's sit here." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "commands", "attached pronoun", "nosotros", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-nuestro-possessive-agreement",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "nuestro/nuestra/nuestros/nuestras + sustantivo",
      "questionEn": "How do you choose the Spanish form of ‘our’ before a noun?",
      "answerEn": "Choose nuestro, nuestra, nuestros, or nuestras to match the gender and number of what is owned.",
      "noteEn": "The form matches the owned noun, not the owners. Use nuestro before one masculine thing, nuestra before one feminine thing, nuestros before masculine or mixed plural things, and nuestras before feminine plural things.",
      "example": { "es": "Nuestras maletas están aquí.", "en": "Our suitcases are here." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "possessives", "agreement", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-para-que-subjunctive-purpose",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "para que + acción deseada",
      "questionEn": "How do you express a purpose when someone or something else carries out the desired result?",
      "answerEn": "Use para que, then put the result verb in the special form used for a purpose that is not yet a fact.",
      "noteEn": "That special form is called the subjunctive. It appears because the second action is intended or desired, not presented as an established fact. For example, ordinary ve becomes vea, and ordinary viene becomes venga. Use para plus an unchanged action verb instead when the same person performs both actions.",
      "example": { "es": "Dejo una nota para que Ana la vea.", "en": "I leave a note so that Ana sees it." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "purpose", "subjunctive", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-como-en-los-viejos-tiempos",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "como en los viejos tiempos",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that an experience feels just like an earlier familiar era?",
      "answerEn": "Use como en los viejos tiempos, meaning ‘just like old times.’",
      "noteEn": "This conventional nostalgic expression compares the present moment with a fondly remembered period. Como supplies the comparison, while los viejos tiempos names the earlier days.",
      "example": { "es": "Volver a jugar juntos fue como en los viejos tiempos.", "en": "Playing together again was just like old times." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "nostalgia", "time", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "concept-goatse-early-web-prank",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "la broma de internet que recuerda la tira",
      "answerEn": "It refers to an infamous early-web shock image that people spread through deceptive links.",
      "noteEn": "The characters imagine future nostalgia for an old trolling ritual: using fake profiles and a social forum to trick strangers into opening an explicit image. The title text warns readers not to search for the reference.",
      "tags": ["concept", "internet history", "shock image", "joke"]
    }
  ],
